module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.by_value_record_widens_container_slots

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data by_value_record_widens_container_slots: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "by_value_record_widens_container_slots" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "The seed emitter fact F (a record whose every field clones without allocating is held by value instead of behind an Rc) took the state-threading specimen from 8116 to 4114 allocator operations (native-allocation-observation, n=2000) and was read as a win. Run end to end on a representative governed arm it was a regression: gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:value-position-block-nests, prebuilt base (5ef31256) against head (5121c34a) built in one dispatch, 9 alternating runs, case reports equal (the arm discards the normalized value), peak RSS equal (~15.6 MB): median 0.637 s at base, 0.752 s at head, and 5.644G against 7.247G instructions under callgrind (+28%).",

    "THE MECHANISM, located by callgrind rather than assumed: the profile localized the added work to two flipped records, v2_compiler_parse LookaheadKey (two Rc fields) (16 B by value, 8 B as an Rc) and LookaheadPrefix (two Rc fields and a Bool) (24 B), both ELEMENTS of im::Vector, whose chunks hold 64 inline slots, so a chunk allocation and every copy-on-write scale with sizeof(element): 512 B at base, ~1 KB and ~1.5 KB at head. The malloc family (libc _int_malloc 137M -> 780M, memcpy 650M -> 972M, chunk free paths) was ~1.2G of the 1.6G increase; the rest of the program matched to ~0.1%. That a chunk crossing glibc's ~1032 B tcache limit is the dominant term is an inference from that size arithmetic and the _int_malloc jump, not a measured allocation-size histogram. The refcount arithmetic (a by-value clone is one increment per Rc field, an Rc clone is one) is real but not the dominant term.",

    "THE NARROWED RULE AND WHY THE IMPLEMENTATION WAS WITHDRAWN: width(R) <= width(Rc<R>) addresses element width only and is not a nonregression theorem. Restricting the clone-allocation-free candidates to non-increasing element width excludes the specimen records (SpecCursor and SpecAcc are 16 B and wider). The about 60 of the 372 flipped records that remain are single-handle newtypes (an Rc around a Vec, HashMap, Node or DeclarationRef) with no demonstrated benefit on the measured arms. We therefore withdrew the implementation rather than retaining it for an unestablished benefit. An ownership-based route to the specimen's allocation reduction (S, linear unwrap of a dying owned record) is a candidate to test, not an established win.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: an allocation-only improvement does not establish a runtime improvement. When a representation change widens elements held in containers, assess the affected container paths, including copying and allocation behavior. A runtime win requires evidence from prebuilt variants executing those uses; its scope is the measured workload.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
